<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph II, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1741">1741</a>–<a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1765, co-ruler with his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>, and sole ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a> from 1780 to 1790. An enlightened absolutist, he abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, granted toleration to Protestants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, closed many monasteries and imposed German as the language of administration. His centralising reforms provoked revolts in the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and many were withdrawn at his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Joseph II, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton von Maron · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_von_Maron_006.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph was born on 13 March 1741 in the Hofburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis I</a>, and the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> and Leopold II. He had 15 siblings. Tutors chosen by his mother gave him a structured education that included much history. In June 1760 he married Isabella of Parma, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, in a match tied to the Austro-French alliance of 1756; they had a daughter, Maria Theresa. As co-ruler he sat on the State Council and wrote minutes for his mother that contain the germs of his later policy, favouring religious toleration, a weaker Church and relief of peasants from feudal burdens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s death on 29 November 1780 left Joseph free to pursue his own policy. He abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1781, issued the Patent of Toleration that year giving limited freedom of worship, secularised church lands, made elementary education compulsory and, in 1784, ordered German to replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as the language of instruction. He issued over 6,000 edicts and 11,000 new laws, and made German the compulsory language of official business, which especially affected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He refused to be crowned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a>, because he would have had to pledge to uphold its rights, and is known there as the Hat-wearing King.</p><p>In 1784 the Allgemeines Krankenhaus opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, though centralisation worsened sanitation and caused a 20 per cent death rate in the new hospital. In foreign policy he twice tried to acquire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, in 1778 and 1785, and was thwarted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a>. He made an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1781, which drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> into the costly and largely futile Austro-Turkish War of 1787–1791.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Joseph fell ill in the autumn of 1788, probably with pulmonary tuberculosis, and died on 20 February 1790, just short of 49. He was buried in a plain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coffin in the Kapuzinergruft in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Leopold II</a>. His policies are known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephinism">Josephinism</a>, and he was a patron of the arts who commissioned Mozart's Die Entführung aus dem Serail. Forged letters, the false <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> letters, long made him seem a more radical philosophe than he probably was.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Roman Catholicism was the dynasty's faith and the established church, although Protestants, Orthodox Christians and Jews were tolerated under the reforms of <b>Joseph II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Joseph II</b> tried to override the Joyous Entry, the Brabant Revolution of 1789 drove out the Austrians and proclaimed the United Belgian States in January 1790, but Austrian troops returned by the end of that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Maria Theresa and <b>Joseph II</b> reforming ministers modernised taxation and administration, and in 1786 the old Senate was abolished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Maria Theresa’s Urbarium of 1767 regulated Hungarian peasant obligations, and <b>Joseph II</b> abolished personal serfdom in the Bohemian lands in 1781 and in Austria in 1782, though labour dues on the land remained.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1620 the Counter-Reformation restored a Catholic monopoly, and a new, partly foreign nobility built baroque estates worked by serfs, whose labour obligations remained heavy until <b>Joseph II</b> abolished personal serfdom in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Habsburgs its separate institutions were steadily reduced: the Renewed Land Ordinance of 1628 subordinated the estates, the reforms of Maria Theresa and <b>Joseph II</b> centralised taxation and administration in Vienna, and from 1782 until the mid-nineteenth century Austrian Silesia was governed together with Moravia from Brno.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1772" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Joseph II, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4231 5922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4231 5922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1772">What was here in 1772? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
